Z||000000||DjKid||08-14-2000||08:14 PM||||That car is beautiful, I swear I love it. but its driving me INSANE! Here's what happened, All was normal, I turned my park lights on and blew the fuse to my instrument panel lights  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> and my altnator light came on.. my guess is a short in my lights somewhere.. fixed the one thing I figured it was, no luck... so tomorrow I'm gonna go through the entire light stuff(or try) and see if any shorts are happening.. my question is.. where would be the easiest or best place to start looking? I NEED MORE HELP!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>DjKid - 88 Coupe<BR>'Blue Knight"||||reg||8
Z||000001||Taijiguy||08-14-2000||08:47 PM||||I'd pull the switch, use the schematics in the Haynes manual to identify the grounds so I could eliminate those. Then I'd jumper from the 12v positive supply at the connector, to all the other terminals one at a time (skipping the ground contacts) until the fuse blew. Then you can identify the circuit with the problem by the color and pin number of the wire. The alternative would be to pull ALL the bulbs in the circuit and do a continuity test to ground. But you would HAVE to remove all the bulbs, since they will appear as dead shorts. The jumpering process is alot easier, and tells you for sure you have a true ground in the circuit someplace. If you jumper all the connections, and the fuse doesn't blow, plug the switch back in and turn the lights on, if the fuse blows, it's probably in the switch itself.<BR>Do a ground check on it and see what you get.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
